;;; A written `(absent Bob)` adds `Bob` to the program's names, and H grows —
;;; M1f **W80**, register row `W80/absent-fact-name`, filed by S1f.7.7 (T1f.7.7.2,
;;; 2026-11-11).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One guest, Ann, a relation `paid`, and a fact `(absent Bob)`. The blind enumerator
;;; builds H from the program's names.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; No fact is of `absent`: refused at the parse, on every command. Without the fact H
;;; is `(paid Ann)` alone and R is two states.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ──────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `52e05fc1`, release: the program loads and stores `(absent Bob)`; `Bob` is a name of
;;; the program, so H is `(paid Ann)` and `(paid Bob)` and R is four states, exit 0 on
;;; every command.
;;; Refused at load since S1f.7.7 (T1f.7.7.3).

(relation paid Guest :why "{?1} has paid")
(is-a Ann Guest)
(absent Bob)
(query :goal (paid ?g))
